The numerals are used with [[numeral classifier]]s. Distributive numerals are formed by reduplicating the first consonant and vowel, e.g. {{lang|sat-Latn|babar}} 'two each'.

Numbers basically follow a [[decimal|base-10]] pattern. Numbers from 11 to 19 are formed by addition, {{lang|sat-Latn|gɛl}} ('10') followed by the single-digit number (1 through 9). Multiples of ten are formed by multiplication: the single-digit number (2 through 9) is followed by {{lang|sat-Latn|gɛl}} ('10'). Some numbers are part of a base-20 number system. 20 can be {{lang|sat-Latn|bar gɛl}} or {{lang|sat-Latn|isi}}.

{{fs interlinear|lang=sat|indent=3
|ᱯᱮ {} ᱜᱮᱞ { {{indent|10}} } or { {{indent|10}} } (ᱢᱤᱫ) {} ᱤᱥᱤ {} ᱜᱮᱞ
|pe {} gel {} or {} (miˀt) {} isi {} gel
|({{no gloss|3}} × {{no gloss|10}}) {} or {} (({{no gloss|1}}) × {{no gloss|20}} + {{no gloss|10}})
|30}}

===Adpositions===
Santali has a quite large number of postpositional words that can be added to either the bare nominals or to the number suffixes and the definitive marker. Some of them require the genitive case. There are complex forms that use combinations of a postposition and a case suffix.{{sfn|Ghosh|2008|p=48}}

